  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The best answer for this question is: 'There is something in the medium the artist loves'. For myself, the book medium offers me the opportunity to exercise my love of technology in the graphic arts. In my first books, I was i n love with the offset press. Later, I loved phototypography and how you could electronically order the printed page. Whatever else these loves meant to me, they expanded my capabilities as an artist. Any idea I c ould photograph or set in type, I could publish. Because I knew the machinery, I could experiment with the book form. Now I am making books with the microcomputer and dot matrix printer. I love the computer because it is th e direction of the future. The computer has electrified my thinking. My capabalities as an artist have been quadrupled because I can index, compare and join diverse ideas and images. The computer has changed how I think about book s and the book form. Already there are electronic books on the market. These electronic structures are more fluid than traditional books, because access to the material is not rigidly linear. In fact, it may be that the be st experimental fiction of this century has yet to be written. &lt;dt.&gt;</subfield>
